      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To determine the significance of degree of symptom-specificity in the disablement condition in chronic back pain."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Cross-sectional design."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SUBJECTS",
          "content": "All inhabitants of a restricted geographical area of Norway, who had had 8 weeks of sick-leave due to back pain during a 2-year period, were included in this study. Following examination they were diagnosed as having \"specific back pain\" (n=34), \"non-specific back pain\" (n=113) or \"widespread pain\" (n=49)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Functioning of the 3 diagnostic subgroups was described and compared. Functioning assessment was guided by the concepts of the International Classification of Functioning, Disability and Health (ICF): health condition, body function and structure, activity, participation and contextual factors."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Pain components and final participation restriction did not differ among the diagnostic subgroups. However, with increasing symptom-specificity, loss of physical body functions and structures and subsequent activity limitation tended to increase. On the other hand, with decreasing symptom-specificity, mental distress, unfavourable contextual factors and dissatisfaction with various factors of life tend to increase, which may raise the impact of pain on restricting participation in activities."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Functioning description according to the components of the ICF model indicated that the disablement condition in patients with back pain who had been on sick-leave for 8 weeks may appear more complex with decreasing symptom-specificity."
        }
      ]},
